#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128850 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "915a7c2d-3797-5dfa-8d68-85e2546a23d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11778" :question/problem "Consider a plane lamina of mass $2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and center of mass $(0,1),$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $2.5\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{m}^2,$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(0,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-0)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =2.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot\\mathrm{m^2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128851 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "153b828d-545a-58fb-bc81-0d7311726a87" :question/math-academy-id "e-11779" :question/problem "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda(x, y) =2(x^2+y^2),$ measured in $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ $D$'s center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda(r,\\theta) = 2r^2.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi \\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"}] :question/id #uuid "72ccaeab-ad76-50c7-8b51-7736c5270521" :question/math-academy-id "e-12493" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $4 \\, \\textrm{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $24 \\, \\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}^2,$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D.$" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} = \\pi \\cdot 4^2 = 16\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{2}{16\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{8\\pi}\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 24 & =16+2{d}^{2} \\\\ 2{d}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =4 \\\\ d & =2\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "131948d9-d533-5316-bcd3-b7859244892e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12494" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le 2, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 4 \\big\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{3}8\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right) = \\left( \\dfrac{0+2}2, \\dfrac{0+4}2 \\right) = (1,2)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{8}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}3(x-1)^{2}+3(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[3(x-1)^{2}y+(y-2)^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12(x-1)^{2}+16\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[4(x-1)^{3}+16x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4+32-(-4+0)) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =5+{1}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+3 \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98a09cfe-fbf0-5885-86ac-126a24f6b01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126774} {:answer/id #uuid "b34e1ebb-8a2a-526e-ba18-9f0d6d3fde86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126775} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2f948-b06f-5603-ba75-20f76b860b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126776} {:answer/id #uuid "42d00cf6-28ca-5694-bccb-82200f9011a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126777} {:answer/id #uuid "72642d6a-44e7-5f46-9c81-4a2410152980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126778}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca129e4f-bfea-5bce-a18f-8b24e0ad149a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126773}] :question/id #uuid "2270513f-c6f6-52b5-a614-102c61f4f73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163169" :question/problem "Consider a plane lamina of mass $3kg$ and center of mass $(1, 1)$, where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $1.5kg \\cdot m^{2}$, what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(-3, 4)$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c131489-5fa6-5eab-a9db-bc8a1451bd7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126798} {:answer/id #uuid "f8826861-9e52-5539-bb32-f67e3c0483f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126799} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b50603-5087-5782-b036-3870e58ea011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126800} {:answer/id #uuid "42de4b38-a059-5d84-bf8b-d9372b95c699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126801} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1e064f-52de-5822-afc4-840c6155d69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126802}] :answer-field/id #uuid "329972dd-0743-503f-b3b1-1ba800c2b1fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126797}] :question/id #uuid "05ff7fe5-2288-5939-b55a-de668286bd7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163220" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $6kg$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $3m$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $39kg \\cdot m^{2}$, find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
